---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';

const path = '/use-cases/replenishment';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/replenishment');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/replenishment');
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a href="/use-cases" class="link text-sm font-bold">← All use cases</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Plan Shopify replenishment reminders</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-slate-700">A reorder reminder should reflect plausible product use and recent orders, without pretending to know what remains in a customer’s cupboard.</p>
    <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
      <h2>Estimate a window with uncertainty</h2>
      <p>Start from the consumable item, pack quantity and the customer’s actual reorder history where available. Purchase date is not necessarily first use. Larger packs, multiple users, gifts and delivery delays can change the interval. Use language such as ready to reorder rather than stating that supply has run out.</p>
      <h2>Recompute after new information</h2>
      <p>Reset the relevant item’s reminder when a replacement order arrives, and remove canceled or fully returned purchases from the estimate. Decide how partial returns and multi-pack orders affect the schedule. Do not reset every product family because the customer bought an unrelated item.</p>
      <h2>Coordinate subscriptions and stock</h2>
      <p>Check whether an upcoming subscription shipment already covers the same need. A paused subscription may call for account management rather than a new subscription pitch. Link to the appropriate pack and destination; offer a back-in-stock option if unavailable instead of sending a dead reorder link.</p>
      <h2>Evaluate scheduling behavior</h2>
      <p>Ask messaging candidates to show how a scheduled reminder changes when order quantity or subscription status changes. Confirm whether profile merges preserve the most recent qualifying purchase. A calendar delay is easy to describe; maintaining the right item-level state is the selection task.</p>
      <h2>Proposed acceptance exercise</h2>
      <p>Schedule reminders for two consumables, reorder only one, then cancel that reorder. Observe which schedules change and document the intended result. Compare reorder timing and contribution across all assigned customers, with returns and channel costs included. Do not attribute every naturally recurring purchase to the reminder.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
